# Line endings, decided once so three agents on three runtimes cannot
# disagree about them.
#
# Why this file exists: on 2026-08-06 AGENTS.md was written with LF into a
# repository where every other file was CRLF, and a merge-conflict
# resolution left mixed \r\n\r\n\n inside tools/check_web.py. Neither was
# visible in a diff. Claude runs on Windows, Codex runs its own bundled
# runtime, and CI runs ubuntu-latest - three writers, three defaults, and
# nothing that reconciled them.
#
# The repository stores LF. Windows working copies get CRLF on checkout
# and are converted back on commit, so nobody has to think about it. LF in
# the repository is what the Linux CI runner reads, which is the half that
# was silently at risk.
* text=auto

# Explicit where the format cares, rather than trusting detection.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
# robots.txt and the three OFL licenses. Pinned like the rest and still
# copied byte for byte by tools/build_web.mjs: the copy mode is what lets
# this pin land on working trees that were checked out before it, where
# both copies of a .txt are still CRLF and agree with each other.
*.txt   text eol=lf

# _headers (0.9-M1-S3, #329): no extension, so `* text=auto` above
# guesses rather than reading a pin - on a Windows checkout that guess
# produced CRLF and broke a parser expecting LF the same day the file
# was added (tests/route-precedence.test.mjs's own history). A bare
# name rather than a path matches both apps/web/_headers and the copy
# tools/build_web.mjs writes to dist/_headers, which is also copy mode
# and therefore also never normalizes this file's line endings itself.
_headers   text eol=lf

# Binary, never touched. woff2 is here for the same reason the rest
# are: text=auto guesses, and a guess that goes the wrong way on a font
# rewrites bytes inside a compressed container and ships a file no
# browser will parse.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff2 binary

# The run launcher: the sh half stays LF everywhere; the cmd half is
# CRLF, because cmd.exe misparses multi-line blocks under LF.
run     text eol=lf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# tests/ROSTER (0.9-M3-S18, #428): two parallel slices that each append
# one row used to conflict at the door - 0.9-M3-S8 and 0.9-M3-S10 both
# did, on 2026-08-21, and `gh pr update-branch` had to stop for a
# specialist merge-forward. git's own built-in union driver takes both
# sides' added lines instead of writing conflict markers, so that same
# shape merges clean now. What it CANNOT do: de-duplicate a row both
# sides added identically at the SAME position - git collapses that
# case to one copy on its own, before the union driver ever runs. Two
# copies happen only when the identical row lands at two DIFFERENT
# positions, since each side then edits a different hunk and the
# driver keeps both. It also has no notion of the roster's ordering
# rule at all - it just concatenates. tests/run.mjs's roster
# reader is what catches both of those (DUPLICATE REQUIRED ROW /
# DUPLICATE EXCLUSION, and the ordering check), so a union merge that
# produces either is red at the gate, never silent. And GitHub's own
# server-side merge (the "Update branch" button, auto-merge) does NOT
# read this file at all - confirmed against GitHub's own words in
# <https://github.com/orgs/community/discussions/9288> ("GitHub doesn't
# consider user-defined .gitattributes files") - so this driver only
# fires on a LOCAL `git merge`; the door's merge-forward step still has
# to run one by hand before it can push.
tests/ROSTER merge=union
